    {
      "id": "bridge-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?**\n\nA Bridge is an explicit, addressable cross-graph authority or participation boundary. It names a specific external resource — a single node class, a bundle of them, or an entire graph — and states specific permissions the current graph has been granted over it, or is offering to whoever imports it.\n\nBridge exists so that cross-graph coupling is never implicit. A custom node type used anywhere in a graph must trace back to a Bridge that grants it. If it can't, that's not a documentation gap — it's the graph actually lacking the authority it's exercising.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
        "_classBinding": {
          "key": "page-section",
          "sourceRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node",
