    {
      "id": "bridge-boundary",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": 440
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive",
        "body": "- Bridge does not fetch or cache the target's content itself. Resolving a ref into actual nodes is the resolver's job (`GraphCrud.js`'s `bridgeReference()`), triggered by an action on the Bridge, not performed by the Bridge node as data.\n- Bridge does not draw the workspace connector line by itself, and that line is not a form of persistence — it only exists while both graphs happen to be open together (see Behavior).\n- Legacy node-type dependency lists grant no authority. Import Bridges and class bindings supply the current authority path.\n- Bridge is not Portal. Portal is a consumer-side navigation/expansion opening into another graph's interface; Bridge is an authority or participation grant. The two can point at the same `ref` for entirely different reasons and neither implies the other.\n- `resourceKind: library` is a real, declared option in `BRIDGE_KINDS`, and the create menu reads one: an export of this kind hands on the classes its `exposure` lists. The validator's authority check does not read it, exactly as it would not read an unkeyed `node-class` bridge. Treat it as partly supported; an export of the graph itself (`resourceKind: graph`) with a class list is the form to author.",
